     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Semi-reproducible example. Based on this issue coming up a couple of years ago, this may work, but it doesn&#39;t:</div><div><br></div><div>ui = tagList(</div><div>  tags$head(includeScript(&quot;google-analytics.js&quot;)),</div><div>  navbarPage(</div><div>    &quot;Title&quot;,</div><div>    tabPanel(title = &quot;First&quot;),</div><div>    tabPanel(title = &quot;Second&quot;)</div><div>  )</div><div>)</div><div>server = function(input, output, session) {}</div><div>shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "1U0x2JhaAwAJ",
   "from": "Bernhard Klingenberg",
   "author": "674973fc58ddf600",
   "date": "2017-06-16T17:22:22Z",
   "subject": "Re: Google Analytics tracking with navbarPage layout",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Actually, this works just fine:<div><br></div><div><div>shinyUI(</div><div>  navbarPage(</div><div>    title=&quot;A Title&quot;,</div><div>    header=singleton(tags$head(includeScript(&quot;google-analytics.js&quot;))),</div></div><div>    ...</div><div><br></div><div>My problem was not with the shiny code but with the Google Analytics script. </div><div><br></div><div>Instead of copying and pasting (from Chrome under Windows 7, and removing the script tags) to create the file, I copied and pasted the code from the RStudio tutorial mentioned above and then simply filled in my tracking number manually. Although the files are identical, Shiny would except the latter but not the former. No idea why.  </div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
